  "live_records": {
    "q-34618": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34618",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $r(0)$ if the curve and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{2t},\\,t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{(2{e}^{2t}+1)^{2}}⟨{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{3t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,-2{e}^{2t}⟩\\,.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(2{e}^{0}+1)^{2}}⟨{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,-2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{9}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{9}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{3}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
