    "q-97585": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97585",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(2,2)$ where $t=2$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}}{2},\\,\\frac{2-t}{2}⟩.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(2)=⟨2,2⟩,$ since the point $P(2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(2)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2-t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2-t}{2\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =⟨\\frac{2-2}{2\\sqrt{4(2)-{2}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=2$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2)+R(2)N(2) & =⟨2,2⟩+2⟨0,-1⟩=⟨2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
