    "q-97594": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97594",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨\\pi ,2⟩$,  since the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩+4⟨0,-1⟩=⟨\\pi ,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
